One module, two bindings (`header` and `bar`) carrying the *identical*
interpolated declaration `background-color: $(Color.Background.Alt.box)`.

Each block gets its own class, and the two blocks share one variable, as on
main: the variable seed is the module plus the block's interpolated
declarations, the path and the type, which are the same here. Each
`CSS.make` sets that variable, so merging the two blocks gives the value of
the later operand.

  $ refmt --parse re --print ml input.re > output.ml
  $ ../../standalone.exe --impl output.ml -o output.ml
  $ styled-ppx.generate output.ml > styles.css

The same declaration body gives two classes and one variable:

  $ cat styles.css
  /* This file is generated by styled-ppx, do not edit manually */
  @property --vdivn44{syntax:"*";inherits:false;}
  .css-vdivvvx{background-color:var(--vdivn44);}
  .css-vdivug2{background-color:var(--vdivn44);}
